The academic sweep is still out, but I secured the key CHI 2009 numbers myself. Here is the report.

---

# Social / guardian recovery as actually practiced and measured

**Method note (affects every confidence rating below).** The session's WebSearch budget was exhausted at the first call, so all of this was gathered by fetching primary URLs directly and by routing search-engine result pages through a text proxy (`r.jina.ai` over DuckDuckGo HTML). Google, Bing, Brave (after ~4 calls), Mojeek, Ecosia, Startpage and Reddit were all bot-walled from this machine. `web.archive.org` was unreachable. **The practical consequence: I could reach primary sources and academic PDFs well, but forum/complaint-thread evidence and pre-2020 removed pages poorly.** Several DuckDuckGo results were AI-generated content farms (`eco.com`, `chainscorelabs.com`, `nadcab.com`, `whysogeek.com`, `ibex.fi`, `stealthcloud.ai`) — I excluded all of them and every number below traces to a primary source or a peer-reviewed paper.

---

## PART 1 — MEASURED NUMBERS

### 1.1 The only rigorous end-to-end measurement of guardian recovery that exists (2009)

Schechter, Egelman, Reeder, *"It's Not What You Know, But Who You Know: A Social Approach to Last-Resort Authentication"*, **CHI 2009**, Microsoft Research. Free PDF: https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/wp-content/uploads/2016/02/paper1459-schechter.pdf — deployed as a real-looking system at `recover.live.com`, 4 trustees, threshold 3-of-4. **This is 17 years old and remains the best measurement in the literature.** All numbers below are quoted from the PDF I fetched. *Confidence: HIGH.*

**Experiment 1 — reliability (the enrollment→completion funnel).** 43 invitees:

| Drop-out stage | n | Remaining |
|---|---|---|
| Invited | — | 43 |
| Never loaded the instructions page | 13 | 30 |
| Never responded to any survey request (despite a $10 gift card offered just to reply) | 4 | 26 |
| **Couldn't remember who their trustees were, and didn't look them up** | 2 | 24 |
| Too busy to attempt the task | 2 | 22 |
| Worried trustees would fear viruses in the emails | 1 | 21 |
| Never spoke with any trustee | 2 | 19 |
| Configured invalid emails for all trustees | 1 | 18 |
| Only succeeded with 2 of the required 3 trustees | 1 | 17 |

**The headline the paper reports is "17 of the 19 participants who made the effort to reach one trustee (89%) were able to authenticate."** That 89% is a *conditional* rate. The unconditional rate is **17/43 = 40% of enrolled users completed recovery**. The paper's own stated lesson: *"users must be reminded of who their trustees are."*

**Experiment 2 — forged-email attack on trustees.** An attacker email soliciting recovery codes reached **89 trustees**. **4 (≈4%) sent an account-recovery code to the attacker** (one of whom then phoned the victim, having second thoughts). **38 alerted the account holder** through another channel. Using those rates the authors compute attack success at **0.46% against a 2-code threshold and 0.019% against a 3-code threshold**.

**Experiment 3 — phone-based attack by someone close to the victim.** 9 participant pairs; calls reached 49 trustees, 7 discarded. **Of the remaining 42 calls, 19 (45%) got the caller an account-recovery code or a trustee-authentication email.** Refusal breakdown: 5 (12%) unwilling, 14 (33%) unable, 4 (10%) too confused. The authors concede the pairs were mostly spouses/long-term friends and call this **"the upper bound for attack efficacy."**

**So: guardians resist a cold email (4%) and fold to a warm phone call (45%).** That asymmetry is the single most load-bearing measured fact in this whole field, and nothing published since supersedes it.

### 1.2 The largest-scale measurement of "can the enrolled user reach their recovery factor" (Google, 2019)

Google Security Blog, **17 May 2019**, *"New research: How effective is basic account hygiene at preventing hijacking"* — https://security.googleblog.com/2019/05/new-research-how-effective-is-basic.html (with NYU and UC San Diego; year-long study). *Confidence: HIGH — quoted verbatim from Google's own post.*

- Recovery phone number blocks **"up to 100% of automated bots, 99% of bulk phishing attacks, and 66% of targeted attacks."** SMS to recovery phone: 100% / 96% / 76%. On-device prompt: 100% / 99% / 90%.
- **The number that matters for recovery design, verbatim: *"In an experiment, 38% of users did not have access to their phone when challenged. Another 34% of users could not recall their secondary email address."***

That 38% / 34% is the closest thing anyone has published to an availability rate for a recovery factor at planetary scale, and it is for the *easiest possible* factors — your own phone, your own backup email. A human guardian is strictly harder to reach than your own phone.

- Also from the same post: "hack for hire" groups charge **on the order of $750 USD** per account, and persist with follow-on spear-phishing **"for upwards of a month."** That is the price of the 45%-success phone call in §1.1.

### 1.3 Ethereum smart-contract wallets

**Loopring — the only fully-documented guardian-recovery breach with denominators.**
Primary: Byron @ Loopring, *"Postmortem Incident Report and Path Forward"*, Medium, **published 2024-10-11** — https://medium.com/@byron.loopring/postmortem-incident-report-and-path-forward-aa64f7e5f7d1 (I fetched the full text). *Confidence: HIGH.*

- Incident #1, **2024-04-24**: attacker compromised Loopring L1/L2 operator keys via the `secretManager`; an admin key "was not strictly managed, and several internal members had access to it." Loopring reset keys but **"did not conduct a thorough review of the backend environment."**
- Incident #2, **2024-06-09**: the **Loopring 2FA server** was compromised (read/write). The attacker **swapped affected users' 2FA email addresses for their own**, initiated social recovery, received the verification code, and Loopring's relayer **"signed 'YES' to the social recovery request"** — transferring wallet ownership to the attacker on-chain, exactly as designed.
- **Denominators, quoted: "Out of 40,000+ Loopring Smart Wallets currently deployed, 58 Loopring Smart Wallet addresses lost assets."** ≈**0.145%** of deployed wallets. Loss ≈**$5M** (The Block / Unchained, 2024-06-09/10).
- **The root cause is the adoption failure, stated by the vendor:** *"Most impacted wallets relied solely on the default Loopring Official Guardian, which only uses users' 2FA information for verification."* Four wallets that *did* have a second guardian were also drained **because that second guardian was itself a Loopring Smart Wallet whose only guardian was the Official Guardian.** Loopring's post-hoc guidance: *"the Loopring wallet is only considered secure when users opt for multiple trusted guardians (minimum 3)."*
- Malware traced to Loopring's AWS servers from **2024-04-19**. Singapore police declined the report (no Singaporean victims; server in the US), so KYC tracing failed. **No reimbursement was announced** — the post says end users "are responsible for their own self-custodial security" and pivots the company to "Loopring DeFi."
- `Loopring/protocols` last pushed **2024-10-25** (via GitHub API).

**Argent — the vendor's own docs collapse the "social" quorum to 1-of-1.**
Argent's own guardian FAQ, fetched live: https://support.argent.xyz/hc/en-us/articles/360007981757-Ethereum-account-guardian-FAQ. *Confidence: HIGH — verbatim.*
- *"If my only guardian is Argent, can I recover my wallet? **Yes. You only need one guardian for a recovery.** Please ensure you have at least one guardian."*
- *"How many guardians do you recommend? This is completely your choice. **One guardian is sufficient** for recovery, locking and approving large transactions… however with more guardian approvals at once, the transaction might become **more expensive fees-wise**."*

That is the whole story in two answers: the default guardian is **Argent itself, verified by SMS**, and the vendor actively discourages adding more because **L1 gas made each extra guardian cost money**. Loopring's 58 drained wallets are what this configuration looks like when the vendor's own guardian server is breached.

Recovery mechanics (https://support.argent.xyz/hc/en-us/articles/360007338877, last seen dated 2025-05-06): **48-hour recovery delay**; guardian types = Argent (SMS), other Argent users, MetaMask, Ledger, Trezor; approval threshold 1 guardian → 1 approval, 2–3 → majority, 4+ → ceil(N/2). *Confidence: HIGH.*

**Argent's fate.** Argent rebranded to **Ready** in late 2025 (X handle `@argentHQ` → `@ready_co`). The wallet did **not** shut down and guardian recovery still exists in the support docs. But: `argentlabs/argent-contracts` (the L1 guardian contract suite, 600 stars) **last pushed 2024-07-29** — roughly two years without a commit. `argentlabs/argent-x` last pushed **2025-03-14**. Ready's live blog (fetched 2026-09) leads with **"Introducing Community Finance," Aug 6 2026** — no social-recovery messaging. The 2020 manifesto URL `argent.xyz/blog/a-new-era-for-crypto-security/`, which the support FAQ still links to as the canonical explanation of guardians, **now 404s to the blog index**. *Confidence: HIGH on repo dates and the dead URL (both directly observed); MEDIUM on the rebrand date (search-summary, not a fetched announcement).*

Separately, and **LOW-MEDIUM confidence** because I could not fetch the primary article: The Defiant reportedly covered Ready shutting down its **card program on 2026-07-30** after issuer Kulipa wound down without warning, with ~1 hour of user notice; wallet assets stayed in self-custody. Treat as unverified.

**Safe{Wallet} — the "social" tier was never built.**
Safe's own help doc, *"Account recovery with Safe{RecoveryHub}"*: https://help.safe.global/articles/9622260218-account-recovery-with-saferecoveryhub. *Confidence: HIGH — verbatim.*
- *"**As of December 2023 the only implemented solution is a decentralized Custom solution**, so this article focuses on that."* The trusted-third-party tier (Sygnum / Coincover) was announced but not shipped at that point.
- A **Recoverer** is **a single account** — no N-of-M threshold at all. *"Recoverers can bypass any threshold and propose ownership changes."* The only defence is a delay window during which the remaining signers can reject.
- Safe's own honest framing of the trustless option: *"The only downside of this trustless approach is reliance on the Recoverer account(s) and the safety of their private key. There is no centralized authority to help you in case the Recoverer's key is lost."* — i.e. the recovery mechanism has the same loss problem as the thing it recovers.
- Underlying primitive: `gnosis/zodiac-modifier-delay`, **31 stars**, last pushed 2026-06-08.

**Ethereum Foundation is still on v1 — with 8 stars.**
Privacy & Scaling Explorations (PSE, Ethereum Foundation), *"Social Recovery SDK: design, implementation and learnings"*: https://pse.dev/blog/social-recovery-sdk. *Confidence: HIGH.*
- Verbatim: *"Social recovery has been discussed for years, but **developers still lack a practical, trustless SDK** they can integrate directly into wallets."* And: *"The topic has been discussed for a long time, including Vitalik Buterin's writing on social recovery in January 2021, **yet there is still no simple, trustless, developer-friendly SDK** that supports multiple authentication methods out of the box."*
- Repo `privacy-ethereum/social-recovery-sdk` (GitHub API): **created 2026-01-12, last pushed 2026-03-02, 8 stars.** 155 passing contract tests. Guardian classes: EOA, Google email via zkJWT, passkeys.
- Their three stated open problems are all *human-coordination* problems, not cryptography: private salt handoff owner↔guardian *"left to the wallet owner (manual handoff)"*; DKIM key rotation with incomplete DNSSEC coverage; and **cross-chain — "users must reconfigure and repeat recovery setup across chains, which degrades UX and increases operational risk… affects real adoption."**
- The adjacent `zkemail/email-recovery` repo: created 2024-05-28, **last pushed 2025-06-25, 61 stars** — stalled ~14 months.

**Five years after Vitalik's post, the Ethereum Foundation's own reference implementation of social recovery has 8 GitHub stars and three unsolved coordination problems.** That is the cleanest available proxy for how the model actually performed.

**Vitalik's 2021 post itself**, https://vitalik.eth.limo/general/2021/01/11/recovery.html, **2021-01-11**: recommends **"high guardian counts (ideally 7+)"**, a 1–3 day delay on guardian changes, and cites the loss motivation ("1500 BTC may be lost every day", "20% of the total supply", Stefan Thomas's 7,000 BTC). It names guardian collusion, relayer centralization, high L1 fees, and guardian-identity disclosure as risks. **Note the gap between "ideally 7+" and Argent's shipped default of 1.** *Confidence: HIGH.*

Its HN thread (https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=25722131, 129 points, 43 comments) is worth reading for the practitioner objection that recurs: several commenters argue N-of-M guardians is not distinguishable from Electrum's cosigner model, and one predicts the guardian role converges on institutions — *"All roads lead to banks."* Loopring and Argent both shipped exactly that (the vendor as default guardian). **HN carries essentially no other discussion of social recovery wallets in 17 years of archive** — searches for "argent wallet", "guardian recovery wallet", "social recovery wallet" return this one thread plus noise. *Confidence: HIGH (Algolia API, full-text).*

### 1.4 Signal Secure Value Recovery

- **SVR1**: https://signal.org/blog/secure-value-recovery/, **2019-12-19**. PIN → Argon2 → auth token + master key, with server-side 256-bit `c2` held in an Intel SGX enclave; enclave enforces a max failed-guess count; Raft across geographically distributed enclaves to prevent counter rollback. Signal's own illustrative parameter: *"if we set the maximum failed guess count to 5…"*. **Contains no user statistics whatsoever.** *Confidence: HIGH.*
- **SVR3**: Connell (Signal), Fang (Berkeley), Schmidt (Signal), Dauterman (Berkeley), Popa (Berkeley), *"Secret Key Recovery in a Global-Scale End-to-End Encryption System"*, **USENIX OSDI 2024** — https://www.usenix.org/conference/osdi24/presentation/connell. Measured: **$0.0025/user/year**, **365ms to recover a key**, deployed to *"millions of real users in a deployment with capacity for over 500 million users"*, n=3 trust domains across three cloud providers (Nitro / SGX / SEV-SNP), s=2, 7 geographically distributed servers, throughput ~1,700 / 1,000 / 3,300 req/s respectively. *Confidence: HIGH.*
- **The critical design admission, verbatim from the paper:** the system *"does not support recovering the user PIN that is used in secret key recovery (i.e., if a user forgets their PIN, they cannot recover their key)."* Their mitigation: *"We mitigate this in practice by **periodically prompting the user to re-enter their PIN on the messaging client** to prevent permanent lockout."*
- **Signal has never published a PIN-forgetting rate.** Not in the 2019 post, not in the 2020 registration-lock post, not in the OSDI 2024 paper. *Confidence: HIGH (I checked all three).* The periodic re-prompt is the strongest indirect evidence that the rate was high enough to engineer around.
- **Critique**: Matthew Green, https://blog.cryptographyengineering.com/2020/07/10/a-few-thoughts-about-signals-secure-value-recovery/, **2020-07-10**. *"The key weakness of the SVR approach is that it depends strongly on the security and integrity of SGX execution."* Three failure modes if SGX falls: seed extraction enabling offline dictionary attack (difficulty *"entirely on the strength of a user's password"*), retry-limit bypass, and replication into simulated non-SGX enclaves if an attestation key leaks — noting attestation signing keys have been extracted **twice**. He catalogs Plundervolt, Foreshadow, ÆPIC. And on consent: *"My major issue with SVR is consent"* — users setting a PIN weren't told it enabled backup; he calls the rollout a *"dark pattern… basically forcing users to add the PIN"* by *"blocking access to the entire app."* He admits picking **"9512"** — a 4-digit PIN, 10,000-space — to make the nag stop. Android Police, **2020-05-29**, https://www.androidpolice.com/2020/05/29/many-signal-users-arent-happy-with-new-pin-requirements/, documents the backlash and Signal's partial climbdown. *Confidence: HIGH.*

### 1.5 Apple and WhatsApp (from the parallel research pass — I have spot-verified the items marked ✔)

**Apple Recovery Contacts** (https://support.apple.com/en-us/HT212513): **up to 5** contacts; both parties on iOS 15+/macOS 12+ with iMessage on, both 13+; the contact relays a **six-digit code**; *"Your recovery contacts won't have any access to your account."* **Apple publishes no time-delay figure and no success rate.** *Confidence: HIGH.*

**Apple Recovery Key** (https://support.apple.com/en-us/HT208072): **28-character** code; **enabling it turns off Apple's standard account-recovery path entirely** — lose the key and all trusted devices and access is permanently gone. Apple warns not to store it in Apple's own apps (circular lockout). **With Advanced Data Protection on, at least one of {Recovery Contact, Recovery Key} is mandatory.** *Confidence: HIGH.*

**iCloud Keychain HSM escrow** (Apple Platform Security guide, https://support.apple.com/guide/security/escrow-security-for-icloud-keychain-sec3e341e75d/1/web/1 — the current restatement of Ivan Krstić's Black Hat 2016 disclosure): HSM clusters, majority quorum, SRP so the security code never reaches Apple. Verbatim: ***"The escrow service allows only 10 attempts to authenticate and retrieve an escrow record"*** — on the 10th, *"the HSM cluster destroys the escrow record and the keychain is lost forever."* And: ***"The administrative access cards that permit the firmware to be changed have been destroyed."*** The original Black Hat PDF (https://www.blackhat.com/docs/us-16/materials/us-16-Krstic.pdf) was fetched but returned as unparseable scanned content. **No peer-reviewed critique of this specific design was located, and no independent verification or dispute of the "cards destroyed" claim was found** — a weak negative given the search constraints. *Confidence: HIGH on the quotes, LOW on the negatives.*

**The Apple failure mode that actually happened in the wild — recovery as the attack.** ✔ WSJ (Nicole Nguyen & Joanna Stern), Feb 2023 and **2023-04-19**, https://www.wsj.com/tech/the-iphone-setting-thieves-use-to-lock-you-out-of-your-apple-account-716d350d; secondary confirmation fetched at https://www.macrumors.com/2023/04/19/apple-responds-to-iphone-theft-recovery-key-report/. Thieves shoulder-surfed passcodes in bars, stole the phone, reset the Apple ID password in Settings, disabled Find My, **then set a Recovery Key** — which permanently locked the legitimate owner out. *"Apple's policy gives users virtually no way back into their accounts without that recovery key."* ✔ Apple's fix was **Stolen Device Protection**, iOS 17.3 (https://support.apple.com/en-us/120340): biometric-only with **no passcode fallback** for sensitive actions, plus a **one-hour security delay** followed by a second biometric check before an Apple Account password change — but **only when away from "familiar locations."** *Confidence: HIGH.*

**No published success or failure rate for Apple Recovery Contacts exists** — not from Apple, not from journalism. **No ADP adoption number has ever been published.** *(Negative results; MEDIUM confidence given search-tool limits.)* Confirmed adjacent fact: Apple **withdrew ADP from the UK entirely in Feb–Mar 2025** rather than comply with a Technical Capability Notice.

**WhatsApp E2E backups** (launched **2021-09-10**): https://blog.whatsapp.com/end-to-end-encrypted-backups-on-whatsapp and https://engineering.fb.com/2021/09/10/security/whatsapp-e2ee-backups/. Design: a **Backup Key Vault** of HSMs across multiple data centers behind ChatD; *"either a password of your choice or a 64-digit encryption key that only you know."* The vault is *"responsible for enforcing password verification attempts and rendering the key permanently inaccessible after a limited number of unsuccessful attempts."* **Meta does not state the attempt count anywhere in its own engineering post** — the widely-repeated "5 attempts" figure could not be verified and should not be cited. **WhatsApp has never published an adoption number for E2E backups.** The security whitepaper PDF was fetched but returned as unparseable binary. *Confidence: HIGH on the quotes, HIGH on the negatives (the engineering post declines the number in its own text).*

### 1.6 Bitcoin multisig (from the parallel research pass)

- **Casa** (casa.io): Standard $21/mo or $250/yr for 2-of-3; Premium $175/mo or $2,100/yr for 3-of-5 with three shipped devices; Private Client offers an optional 6th key. Homepage claims **"$10+ Billion Protected"** and **"40,000+ Clients"** — *marketing, unaudited*.
- **"Casa Covenant" no longer exists as a name** — the product is now plain **"Casa Inheritance"**, with a **6-month verification window** during which Casa notifies the owner of a claim (Private Client "Enhanced Verification," US-only, removes the wait). **No usage statistics on how many inheritance transfers have actually executed.**
- **The "X% of Casa clients have lost at least one key" statistic could NOT be located** despite checking Casa's blog archive back to 2018 and the key-management tag. Treat as **unconfirmed / not found**, not as nonexistent — it may live in a talk or podcast. Likewise the **"we have never lost a client's funds"** phrasing was not found on the current site.
- **Unchained** (unchained.com/about) headlines **"$0 Capital losses"**, **"₿100,000+"** secured, **"$1 Billion+"** loans originated — again self-reported with no methodology or period.
- **Nunchuk** is the only vendor that publishes an honest epistemic caveat, in its Bitcoin inheritance guide (2025-12-09, nunchuk.io/blog/bitcoin-inheritance-guide): ***"it's impossible to know exactly how many of these losses are due to failed inheritance plans."*** Their design splits a *"Smooth Path"* (vendor-assisted) from a **Miniscript on-chain timelock "Failsafe"** that works if Nunchuk itself disappears — the only vendor-independence mechanism I saw anywhere in this research.
- **The descriptor problem is real and standardized-against.** Bitcoin Optech Newsletter #351, **2025-04-25**, covers Salvatore Ingala's proposal for a standardized encrypted **wallet-descriptor backup** so that a holder of one of the descriptor's pubkeys can recover the descriptor from an xpub. The existence of that proposal is the evidence: **holding your seed is not sufficient; losing the descriptor loses the funds.** Nunchuk's own recovery guide treats descriptor/BSMS-file possession as a hard precondition.
- **"~20% of BTC is lost"**: Wikipedia currently sources this to a **WSJ article from 2018**, not to Chainalysis. **The commonly-cited Chainalysis Nov-2020 figure could not be verified** — the Chainalysis blog URLs 404 and archive.org was unreachable. Vitalik's 2021 post repeats "20% of the total supply" without a citation. **Cite this number as folklore.**
- **A dated 2026 hardware incident surfaced that I could not independently verify** and flag as such: Coinkite reportedly disclosed a Coldcard RNG defect on **2026-07-30** (entropy chip skipped; ~40 bits on Mk2/Mk3 firmware 4.0.1–5.0.3, ~72 bits on Mk4/Mk5/Q), with **1,366.58 BTC drained on 30–31 July 2026** per Casa's writeup, and BIP-39 passphrases **not** fully protecting victims. Both Casa and Unchained published advisories; both recommended rotating keys via a **private transaction relay** to avoid mempool front-running during the rotation. **This is secondary reporting from two vendor blogs, corroborating each other but not the primary Coinkite disclosure — MEDIUM confidence.** It is relevant here only as evidence that the mitigation for a compromised key is itself an expert-level operation.
- **No one publishes a multisig-specific or collaborative-custody-specific failure rate.** Only aggregate "% of all BTC ever lost" estimates exist, which conflate death, forgotten passwords, discarded hardware, pre-2013 exchange failures and deliberate burns.

---

## PART 2 — EXPLICIT NEGATIVE RESULTS

These are findings. Every one was hunted for specifically.

1. **No wallet vendor has ever published what fraction of its users configure guardians.** Not Argent/Ready, not Loopring, not Safe. The closest available inference is Loopring's postmortem stating that *"most impacted wallets relied solely on the default"* and that adding real guardians was the exception — plus Argent's FAQ telling users one is enough and more costs gas.
2. **No wallet vendor has ever published how many recoveries were actually performed, attempted, or abandoned.** Zero recovery-success-rate data exists for any production smart-contract wallet.
3. **No guardian non-response rate has ever been published by any deployment.** The only figures in existence are Schechter 2009's funnel and Google's 38%/34% for non-guardian factors.
4. **No longitudinal study exists** measuring whether users who enrolled guardians can complete a recovery months or years later. Schechter's was days. This is the single largest gap in the literature and it has stood open for 17 years.
5. **Signal has never published a PIN-forgetting rate**, despite engineering a periodic re-prompt specifically to suppress it.
6. **WhatsApp has never published an E2E-backup adoption number or its lockout attempt threshold** — Meta's own engineering post declines to state the number.
7. **Apple has never published a Recovery Contact success rate or an ADP adoption number.**
8. **Casa/Unchained/Nunchuk publish no key-loss or recovery-exercise rates.** Only "$0 losses" style marketing.
9. **No Vitalik Buterin retrospective admitting the model underperformed was found.** He has continued writing on account abstraction and wallets, but I found no post revisiting the 2021 claims against outcomes. Absence here is meaningful but not proof — search access was degraded. *(MEDIUM confidence.)*
10. **No documented real-world incident of an attacker socially engineering a human guardian** into approving a wallet recovery was found. Every real incident I could document attacked the **vendor-operated** guardian (Loopring's 2FA server) or the **device passcode** (Apple). *(MEDIUM confidence — searches on this phrasing returned nothing at all.)*
11. **Facebook never published outcome data for Trusted Contacts** in nine years of operation, and published none when killing it.

---

## PART 3 — FAILURE TAXONOMY, AS OBSERVED

Ordered by how much evidence there is, not by how much attention each gets.

**1. Users never enroll, or enroll the degenerate configuration.** *(Strongest evidence.)* Argent's FAQ actively steers users to one guardian and names gas cost as the reason for not adding more. Loopring's 58 drained wallets were overwhelmingly single-default-guardian. Vitalik recommended 7+; the shipped default was 1. **The gap between the recommended and the shipped threshold is where every loss lives.**

**2. The "social" guardian is the vendor, so the model is custodial in fact.** Argent's default guardian is Argent, verified by SMS. Loopring's was Loopring, verified by 2FA email. Safe's Recoverer is a single account with no threshold. **Compromise the vendor's server and you compromise every wallet that took the default** — which is what happened on 2024-06-09. The HN commenters in Jan 2021 called this on day one.

**3. The recovery mechanism becomes the attack vector.** The Apple 2023 case is the cleanest instance: the thief doesn't defeat the Recovery Key, the thief *sets* one. Loopring is the same shape — the attacker doesn't defeat recovery, they *invoke* it. **Any recovery path that can change ownership is an ownership-change path for whoever reaches it first.** The only defence that worked in both cases was a **delay plus an out-of-band alert to the owner** (Argent's 48h, Apple's 1h + biometric, Safe's rejection window, Schechter's 38-of-89 trustees who phoned the victim).

**4. Users forget who their guardians are.** Schechter, verbatim: *"users must be reminded of who their trustees are"* — 2 of 43 dropped out at exactly this step. Signal engineered around the same class of failure with periodic PIN re-prompts. **Nobody in the wallet space ships a guardian liveness check.** Casa's guidance to run a key health-check "about once every six months" is the closest thing, and it is advice, not a mechanism.

**5. Guardians are reachable but unavailable.** Google: 38% couldn't reach their own phone. Schechter: 4 of 43 never responded even for $10; 2 more were "too busy"; 1 configured invalid emails for *all* trustees. **A guardian set is a liveness assumption, and liveness decays silently between enrollment and need.**

**6. Guardians fold to a warm phone call — 45%.** Schechter Experiment 3, upper bound. The cold-email rate was 4%. **The threat model that matters is the person who knows you, not the spammer.** Google prices this attack at ~$750.

**7. Guardian collusion / no threshold at all.** Safe's Recoverer can *"bypass any threshold"* single-handedly. PSE's SDK team identified timelocks as non-negotiable precisely because *"without them, a colluding guardian set can potentially take over a wallet before the owner can react."*

**8. Correlated guardians.** Loopring's four-wallet edge case: users who *did* add a second guardian added another Loopring Smart Wallet — whose only guardian was the Official Guardian. **N-of-M means nothing if the M are not independent, and users cannot see the dependency graph.**

**9. Cost is a security parameter.** Argent's own FAQ: more guardians → *"the transaction might become more expensive fees-wise."* On L1, gas priced people into 1-of-1.

**10. Recovery state doesn't travel.** PSE: *"users must reconfigure and repeat recovery setup across chains, which degrades UX and increases operational risk… affects real adoption."* Same class as the Bitcoin descriptor problem — the *configuration* is a second secret you must not lose, distinct from the key.

**11. The vendor exits.** Facebook's Trusted Contacts — the largest social-recovery deployment in history, launched 2011 as "Trusted Friends", ~2 billion accounts — **was removed in 2022**. Facebook's own help-page notice, quoted in Ask Leo's contemporaneous coverage (https://askleo.com/facebook-trusted-contacts-is-going-away-prepare-now/, ~May 2022; corroborated by azcentral 2022-05-30 and Screen Rant): ***"Trusted contacts is going away soon. This means your chosen friends will no longer be able to help you get back on Facebook if you lose access to your account."*** Their replacement advice: keep your email and phone number current. **Meta ran the largest possible experiment in social recovery, ran it for roughly nine years, published no results, and shut it down.** *Confidence: HIGH on the removal and the quote; MEDIUM on the exact date.*

---

## PART 4 — RECOMMENDATIONS

Separated from the evidence, and each tagged with which measurement drives it.

1. **Assume 1-of-1 is what actually ships, and make that configuration safe.** Every deployment converged on a single default guardian regardless of the recommended threshold. Design so the degenerate case is survivable — Loopring's postmortem asking users to *"implement multiple Guardians"* after the fact is not a mechanism. *(Loopring 58/40,000; Argent FAQ.)*

2. **The delay window plus an out-of-band owner alert is the load-bearing control, not the threshold.** It is the one thing that worked across all three real incident classes. Argent 48h, Apple 1h+biometric, Safe's rejection window, and Schechter's 38-of-89 trustees who broke channel to warn the victim. Make cancellation cheaper and louder than approval. *(§3.3.)*

3. **Ship a guardian liveness check.** Schechter's explicit lesson and Signal's actual production mitigation both point at the same fix, and no wallet does it: periodically re-prove that each guardian is reachable *and* that the owner still remembers who they are. Degrade or re-prompt when a guardian goes cold. *(Schechter; SVR3 periodic PIN re-entry.)*

4. **Treat guardian independence as a first-class, checkable property.** Refuse or warn on guardian sets that share a root of trust — the four Loopring wallets that added a guardian and were drained anyway. Surface the dependency graph to the user at enrollment. *(Loopring edge case.)*

5. **Design the guardian's approval UX against the 45% phone call, not the 4% email.** Approval should require something the caller cannot supply over the phone: a device-bound signature, a fresh out-of-band confirmation to the *owner's* channel, and an explicit "did someone call you about this?" interstitial. The measured refusal reasons were "unable" (33%) more than "unwilling" (12%) — friction is protective here. *(Schechter Exp. 3.)*

6. **Never let the recovery path be reachable from a single stolen credential.** Apple shipped exactly this fix (biometric with no passcode fallback + delay) only after journalists documented the loss. Design it in first. *(WSJ 2023 / iOS 17.3.)*

7. **Back up the configuration, not just the key.** The guardian set, the threshold, the descriptor, the salt — all are secrets whose loss loses the funds, and all are the parts that don't travel across chains or devices. *(PSE open problem #3; Optech #351.)*

8. **Publish your numbers, because nobody else has.** Enrollment rate, guardians-per-user distribution, recovery attempts, recovery completions, time-to-first-guardian-approval, guardian non-response. **Seventeen years after the only real study, the entire field is operating on one 43-person experiment from 2009 and a Google footnote.** Instrumenting this would make you the only source of truth in the field, and it is cheap.

9. **Do not cite "20% of Bitcoin is lost" as established.** It traces to a 2018 WSJ piece via Wikipedia; the Chainalysis figure everyone repeats could not be verified against any live source. Vitalik's 2021 post repeats it uncited, and it has been the motivating number for the entire social-recovery program.

10. **Budget for the vendor exiting.** Facebook killed the largest deployment; Argent's guardian contracts have not been committed to in ~2 years; ZK Email's recovery repo has been idle ~14 months; the EF's own SDK has 8 stars. **Nunchuk's "Failsafe" — an on-chain timelock that works if the vendor disappears — is the only design I found anywhere that treats vendor mortality as a requirement.** Copy it.

---

