The academic sweep landed. It confirms my report's spine and adds four things worth folding in. **Delta only:**

## Corrections and additions to PART 1

**Schechter CHI 2009 — three numbers I did not extract, all of which strengthen the argument:**

- **72% (13 of 18) of the participants who succeeded had to be reminded who their own trustees were.** My report said "users must be reminded of who their trustees are" as a qualitative lesson from the paper's discussion. It is actually a measured majority. This makes **Recommendation #3 (ship a guardian liveness check) the highest-value item in the report**, not the third — nearly three quarters of *successful* recoverers could not name their guardian set unaided, and that was days after enrollment, not years.
- **18 of 19 (94%) would have succeeded at a relaxed 2-of-4 threshold**, vs. 17 of 19 (89%) at 3-of-4. Threshold relaxation buys ~5 points of reliability against the 0.46%-vs-0.019% attack-success tradeoff I quoted.
- **Phone-attack success split by acquaintance: 56% (14/25) when the caller already personally knew the trustee, vs. 29% (5/17) when they didn't** (N too small for significance, but directional). The 45% aggregate I reported is a blend of two quite different threat models.
- Completion timing: 47% under 100 minutes, 66% within a day, 88% within two days. **A 48-hour cancellation window (Argent's) is roughly the full completion distribution** — worth noting when tuning delay length.

Our two independent full-text reads of Experiment 2 agree exactly (4/89 success, 52/89 failure, 33/89 no response), which is a useful cross-check on the extraction.

**New source, and the best longitudinal number in the field:** Bonneau, Bursztein, Caron, Jackson, Williamson, *"Secrets, Lies, and Account Recovery: Lessons from the Use of Personal Knowledge Questions at Google"*, **WWW 2015** — https://static.googleusercontent.com/media/research.google.com/en//pubs/archive/43783.pdf. **11 million account-recovery claims** analyzed. **40% of English-speaking US users could not recall their secret-question answers when they actually needed to recover**, against **>80% success for SMS reset codes**. *Confidence: HIGH (primary PDF read).* This closes part of gap #4 in my negatives list: at 11M-claim scale, **two in five users cannot reproduce a thing they themselves chose and enrolled.** A human guardian is a harder recall problem than your own secret answer.

**Clean negative, now documented:** Brainard, Juels, Rivest, Szydlo, Yung, *"Fourth-Factor Authentication: Somebody You Know"*, **ACM CCS 2006** — the founding paper of the whole guardian idea — **contains no empirical user data at all.** Its own text: *"We are presently in the process of conducting a small pilot study."* Schechter 2009, citing it, confirms: *"To our knowledge, no usability results have been made available."* **The field's originating paper shipped zero measurements, and the follow-up study three years later is still the only one.**

**Confirms my gap #4 and #10:** searching OpenAlex across SOUPS/CHI/USENIX/PETS/CCS/FC/NDSS surfaced **no academic study empirically measuring usage or success rates of guardian-based social-recovery crypto wallets** — only scheme proposals (IEEE Access 2018) and surveys (ACM CSUR 2023). The crypto-wallet usability literature that does exist is qualitative and reports no recovery-failure rate: Voskobojnikov et al. CHI 2021 (45,821 app reviews, 6,859 coded), Mai et al. SOUPS 2020 (N=29), Eskandari et al. USEC 2015 (expert walkthrough, no end users).

## One item where my finding supersedes theirs

The academic agent could not verify the **Facebook Trusted Contacts** removal and rated it 3/20. **I verified it independently** — Facebook's own help-page disclaimer quoted verbatim in contemporaneous coverage (https://askleo.com/facebook-trusted-contacts-is-going-away-prepare-now/, ~May 2022): *"Trusted contacts is going away soon. This means your chosen friends will no longer be able to help you get back on Facebook if you lose access to your account."* Corroborated by azcentral (2022-05-30) and Screen Rant. **Keep that finding at HIGH confidence for the removal and the quote, MEDIUM for the exact date.**

## One number to treat as weak

Krombholz et al., *"The Other Side of the Coin"* (Financial Cryptography 2016, DOI 10.1007/978-3-662-54970-4_33) is widely cited as finding **22% of Bitcoin users lost money to security violations or self-induced errors**. The primary PDF was unreachable (paywalled/404 on every mirror); the 22% comes only from a third party's citation (Bushager et al. 2018). ** — do not cite it as established**, same treatment as the "20% of BTC lost" folklore.

---

**Net effect on the recommendations:** #3 (guardian liveness check / periodic re-prompt) moves to the top. It is now backed by three independent measurements pointing the same way — Schechter's 72% who forgot their trustees within days, Google's 40% who couldn't recall their own secret answers across 11M real recovery claims, and Signal's decision to engineer periodic PIN re-entry into production rather than measure the failure. **Every deployment that measured recall found it collapsing, and no wallet ships a mechanism against it.**
